


@@BEGIN: PRESENTATION
@id: ros-fabregas
@title: Bridging Digital Repositories in the Hispanic World: exploring interoperability between <i>Fondo de Música Tradicional</i> and <i>Books of Hispanic Polyphony</i>
@presenter: ros-fabregas
@abstract: 

After a recent complete technological overhaul of the two
digital platforms <i>Fondo de Música Tradicional IMF-CSIC</i>
(FMT: https://musicatradicional.imf.csic.es/, in open
access since 2012, renewed in 2025) and <i>Books of Hispanic
Polyphony</i> (BHP: https://hispanicpolyphony.eu/, created in
2013, in open access since 2017, and renewed in 2026) there
are new challenges and goals that go beyond the improvement
of interface characteristics and increase of data in each
platform. Finding musical concordances through numeric
incipits in each database is an important task, especially
in BHP in an effort to reduce the current approximate 30%
of anonymous works. Although the first impression is that
the contents and purpose of FMT and BHP seem quite
different, as these digital platforms grew, it became clear
that a <i>longue durée</i> perspective of Hispanic repertoire
—at least from the 15<sup>th</sup> to the 20<sup>th</sup> centuries— favored
connections between written and unwritten music traditions
(both sacred and secular), as well as the close
relationships of certain literary genres (such as
<i>romances</i>/ballads) and their musical counterparts, including
the tradition of <i>se canta al tono de</i> / <i>cantasi come</i> to
trace well known melodies with which to sing multiple
poetic texts. Thus, at some point BHP was connected to FMT
so that, after a search by title of work in BHP, it was
possible to check similar titles, or keywords, in FMT with
just a click without having to move from one database to
the other. This was just the beginning of finding out ways
to establish possible meaningful links between FMT and BHP,
but as AI has entered the scene, the workflow in both
platforms has improved, and certain prompt procedures with
AI agents provide an unexpected help in order to explore
these two large datasets as one in combination with other
repositories. This Conference provides an ideal opportunity
to present several aspects of work-in-progress in this
direction and to receive the feedback and experience from
other projects.

@@END: PRESENTATION



